WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.376
This is Chapter 8: deploying
to test and production.

4
00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:13.038
Each chapter's second lab proves
one provider in isolation.

5
00:00:13.038 --> 00:00:15.982
A deployment proves them
together, on a schedule,

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.412
with a rollback someone has already run.

7
00:00:18.412 --> 00:00:23.032
This chapter is a runbook: one
ordered set of copy-paste commands,

8
00:00:23.032 --> 00:00:27.563
run once against test and again,
unchanged, against production.

9
00:00:27.563 --> 00:00:29.237
Three notes before we begin.

10
00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:32.463
The chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

11
00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:37.887
Nobody has run this runbook against a
live tenant, account, tenancy or project.

12
00:00:37.887 --> 00:00:42.010
And the code is illustrative,
run only in fixture mode.

13
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:46.810
Why a runbook?

14
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.423
Because each earlier lab proves
one provider on its own,

15
00:00:50.423 --> 00:00:54.822
and production needs all of them
together, every week, with a way back.

16
00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:56.473
So the goal is concrete.

17
00:00:56.473 --> 00:01:00.894
By the end you can deploy the kit to a
test environment and then to production,

18
00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:03.561
with a change record, an
approval at each write,

19
00:01:03.561 --> 00:01:08.053
a smoke test whose expected result is
written down before you run it, evidence

20
00:01:08.053 --> 00:01:12.335
the security officer can check with a
hash, and a rollback you've rehearsed.

21
00:01:12.335 --> 00:01:17.160
And for every step you can say which of
the guide's seven gates it moves forward.

22
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.273
Next, which cloud.

23
00:01:22.273 --> 00:01:26.585
Every command reads the cloud from the
settings file, one name per provider.

24
00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:31.949
The estate this runbook writes for is
Microsoft 365 G C C with commercial Azure,

25
00:01:31.949 --> 00:01:35.662
so Graph and Resource Manager
are the worldwide hosts,

26
00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:39.210
as Microsoft Learn's national
cloud page lists them.

27
00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.820
Oracle Cloud takes its
commercial or government realm,

28
00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.685
AWS its commercial or GovCloud partition,
and Google Cloud its global name.

29
00:01:47.685 --> 00:01:50.901
As a side note, G C C High
and the Defense Department

30
00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:54.510
cloud sign in to Graph with
their own environment names.

31
00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:59.100
Any other name throws, and a session
signed in elsewhere is refused.

32
00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:05.550
The runbook doesn't decide
what the gates decide.

33
00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.238
It turns them on in an
order where each step

34
00:02:08.238 --> 00:02:10.925
can be undone before
the next depends on it.

35
00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:13.300
Reads first, one provider at a time.

36
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.287
Routing as a dry run
before anything is sent.

37
00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:21.325
The one write to a cloud, the Azure
alert policy, only after the reads work.

38
00:02:21.325 --> 00:02:22.763
And the schedule last.

39
00:02:22.763 --> 00:02:25.374
That order is the
workbook's design guidance.

40
00:02:25.374 --> 00:02:27.388
Every collector call is a read.

41
00:02:27.388 --> 00:02:32.437
The few writes run only with an approved
plan hash, or with an explicit send flag.

42
00:02:32.437 --> 00:02:35.300
And three things aren't
in the kit: the schedule,

43
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:38.210
the sign-ins, and the ServiceNow endpoint.

44
00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.883
One settings file per stage holds
every value the commands need,

45
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.622
and the kit checks it
before any command runs.

46
00:02:48.622 --> 00:02:52.210
No secrets: a secret-like name
with a value stops the run,

47
00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:57.073
and the ServiceNow token is read at
run time from an environment variable.

48
00:02:57.073 --> 00:03:00.085
No placeholders left in
an enabled provider.

49
00:03:00.085 --> 00:03:02.373
The file must be the stage it says it is.

50
00:03:02.373 --> 00:03:05.834
Each enabled provider names
who receives its notices today.

51
00:03:05.834 --> 00:03:09.872
And moving from test to production
changes the file and nothing else.

52
00:03:09.872 --> 00:03:14.395
To start, copy the template, fill it
in, and check it: you get one line

53
00:03:14.395 --> 00:03:19.630
with the stage, the change record,
the output folder and the file's hash.

54
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.728
The runbook's scripts are PowerShell,

55
00:03:25.728 --> 00:03:28.455
like the rest of the kit,
and each has a dry run.

56
00:03:28.455 --> 00:03:31.430
The pre-flight checks
everything and changes nothing.

57
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:35.559
The alert policy script plans an
assignment and a remediation,

58
00:03:35.559 --> 00:03:38.180
and applies only with the approved hash.

59
00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:43.354
The scheduled run collects every enabled
provider, routes, writes the digest,

60
00:03:43.354 --> 00:03:45.295
and records each as evidence,

61
00:03:45.295 --> 00:03:49.418
and it sends only when both the
settings and the command say so.

62
00:03:49.418 --> 00:03:53.184
The rollback plans in reverse
order: unregister the schedule,

63
00:03:53.184 --> 00:03:58.280
delete the assignment, and list what the
remediation created, for the change record.

64
00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.050
Twenty-five tests cover
them, all in fixture mode.

65
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:09.375
Before any tenant, run the whole
thing on the kit's fixtures.

66
00:04:09.375 --> 00:04:13.115
It takes the fictional test stage
end to end, then the production

67
00:04:13.115 --> 00:04:16.925
pre-flight with the test evidence,
and nothing leaves the machine.

68
00:04:16.925 --> 00:04:22.162
This output is real: the kit
printed it on November 9, 2026.

69
00:04:22.162 --> 00:04:26.125
The scheduled run collected
fifteen notices from Azure,

70
00:04:26.125 --> 00:04:30.676
Microsoft 365 and Oracle Cloud,
routed eight new tasks as a dry run,

71
00:04:30.676 --> 00:04:34.975
and wrote a digest with thirteen
open and five needing action.

72
00:04:34.975 --> 00:04:38.347
The stage check found every
file matching its hash,

73
00:04:38.347 --> 00:04:41.800
an unbroken chain, and a
passing record for each step.

74
00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:45.170
The production pre-flight
passed twenty-three checks.

75
00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.649
The pre-flight reads only,

76
00:04:50.649 --> 00:04:54.520
and you run it before each stage,
after the readers sign in.

77
00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.620
It checks the settings and routing files,
that each provider's tool is installed,

78
00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.174
that each session is signed in
to the right cloud, tenant,

79
00:05:03.174 --> 00:05:06.883
partition or realm, and that
each reader's first call works.

80
00:05:06.883 --> 00:05:11.107
For AWS that includes the support
plan the health service needs.

81
00:05:11.107 --> 00:05:14.839
It checks that someone is named to
receive each provider's notices,

82
00:05:14.839 --> 00:05:17.145
and that the output folder can be written.

83
00:05:17.145 --> 00:05:20.380
And in production only,
the seventh check refuses

84
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:23.830
to go on unless the test
stage's evidence passes.

85
00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:28.505
Who does what?

86
00:05:28.505 --> 00:05:31.713
The operator runs the
commands and holds no write

87
00:05:31.713 --> 00:05:34.530
role in any cloud except
where a step says so.

88
00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:40.089
Each provider has its own reader with
the least privilege its chapter's

89
00:05:40.089 --> 00:05:45.743
second lab grants: Azure, Microsoft 365,
Oracle Cloud, AWS and Google Cloud.

90
00:05:45.743 --> 00:05:48.743
The one write role in
Azure belongs to a person,

91
00:05:48.743 --> 00:05:51.192
activated only for the change window.

92
00:05:51.192 --> 00:05:52.866
Approvals are written down:

93
00:05:52.866 --> 00:05:56.648
the change advisory board approves
each stage's change record,

94
00:05:56.648 --> 00:06:00.140
a reviewer who isn't the operator
approves each plan's hash,

95
00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:03.631
and the security officer and
the board approve promotion.

96
00:06:03.631 --> 00:06:06.690
Turning on sending waits
for the change manager.

97
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:14.355
Stage 1 starts by signing in
each enabled provider's reader,

98
00:06:14.355 --> 00:06:16.752
in the cloud the settings file names.

99
00:06:16.752 --> 00:06:22.614
For a G C C tenant that's the commercial
Azure cloud and Graph's global environment.

100
00:06:22.614 --> 00:06:25.011
Connecting to Graph with a client ID,

101
00:06:25.011 --> 00:06:28.925
a tenant ID and a certificate
thumbprint is app-only access,

102
00:06:28.925 --> 00:06:33.877
as Microsoft Learn describes it, the
same sign-in the scheduled run will use.

103
00:06:33.877 --> 00:06:38.253
The AWS caller's identity should
start with the partition's prefix.

104
00:06:38.253 --> 00:06:41.315
Then run the pre-flight
and record it as evidence.

105
00:06:41.315 --> 00:06:46.090
Expected: every check passes, and
the first evidence record passes.

106
00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:50.580
Fix any failure before going on:
the detail names what's missing.

107
00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:57.243
Step 1.3 is the staged enable.

108
00:06:57.243 --> 00:06:59.634
Read each enabled provider on its own,

109
00:06:59.634 --> 00:07:04.572
cross-check it against the provider's
own command, which each chapter's second

110
00:07:04.572 --> 00:07:09.355
lab gives, and record it as evidence,
before the providers ever run together.

111
00:07:09.355 --> 00:07:13.818
Start with the one the agency relies
on most: that's design guidance.

112
00:07:13.818 --> 00:07:17.213
Expected: each collection
prints its provider's row,

113
00:07:17.213 --> 00:07:19.818
and its count matches the provider's own.

114
00:07:19.818 --> 00:07:23.280
An empty list is a valid
result in a test environment.

115
00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:27.918
But an empty list where the provider's
own command finds notices is a failure.

116
00:07:27.918 --> 00:07:31.635
Azure, Microsoft 365
and Oracle Cloud first;

117
00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:35.400
then AWS and Google Cloud where enabled.

118
00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.194
Step 1.4 routes the full collection with

119
00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:45.238
the agency's own routing
file, as a dry run.

120
00:07:45.238 --> 00:07:48.838
The service desk reads the
payloads before anything is sent.

121
00:07:48.838 --> 00:07:54.226
Expected: payloads written, nothing sent;
every security advisory and retirement

122
00:07:54.226 --> 00:07:58.900
has one, and each assignment group is
one the change manager recognizes.

123
00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:01.624
Step 1.5 is the smoke test: four checks,

124
00:08:01.624 --> 00:08:06.163
each with its expected result
written down before you run it.

125
00:08:06.163 --> 00:08:09.948
Every provider read; a second
routing writes nothing new;

126
00:08:09.948 --> 00:08:13.250
every payload valid; and
the evidence chain clean.

127
00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:18.760
On the fixtures, the first routing created
eight tasks and the second created none.

128
00:08:22.510 --> 00:08:25.673
Step 1.6 is the one write to a cloud.

129
00:08:25.673 --> 00:08:29.984
In the test settings, the alert policy's
scope is the test subscription.

130
00:08:29.984 --> 00:08:33.385
Read the real built-in
definition first, then plan.

131
00:08:33.385 --> 00:08:36.822
A reviewer who isn't the operator
approves the plan's hash,

132
00:08:36.822 --> 00:08:40.185
and the policy writer applies
it with exactly that hash.

133
00:08:40.185 --> 00:08:44.310
Expected: both steps applied
and the remediation complete.

134
00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:49.159
Microsoft Learn says the remediation
creates a resource group holding the Service

135
00:08:49.159 --> 00:08:53.523
Health alert rule, with an action group
that emails the subscription owners.

136
00:08:53.523 --> 00:08:55.180
Don't edit that rule by hand:

137
00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:59.070
change the assignment's
parameters and remediate again.

138
00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.608
Step 1.7 puts the weekly
run on a schedule.

139
00:09:06.608 --> 00:09:09.210
The kit doesn't register
a schedule itself,

140
00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:12.260
so this uses Windows Task
Scheduler's own cmdlets,

141
00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:17.244
as Microsoft Learn documents them, with
the task name the rollback later removes.

142
00:09:17.244 --> 00:09:21.398
The scheduled run needs every
reader's sign-in in its own session,

143
00:09:21.398 --> 00:09:25.003
so you write a small sign-in
script for the run-as account,

144
00:09:25.003 --> 00:09:28.608
run it once by hand, then register
it weekly, Monday at six.

145
00:09:28.608 --> 00:09:32.695
Expected: collect, route
and digest each pass.

146
00:09:32.695 --> 00:09:36.197
One gap is stated plainly:
how each other provider's

147
00:09:36.197 --> 00:09:40.033
tool signs in unattended is
not yet specified in the kit.

148
00:09:40.033 --> 00:09:42.670
Then send the digest to the owners.

149
00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.952
Step 1.8 checks the stage's evidence:
every file matches its hash,

150
00:09:50.952 --> 00:09:56.095
the chain is unbroken, and each step
the stage needs has a passing record.

151
00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:59.070
Step 1.9 rehearses the rollback.

152
00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:03.570
Plan it, have the reviewer approve
its hash, apply it and record it.

153
00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:07.852
Expected: the schedule unregistered,
the policy assignment deleted,

154
00:10:07.852 --> 00:10:12.135
and the third step left manual: a
list of what the remediation created,

155
00:10:12.135 --> 00:10:14.238
for the change record to decide on,

156
00:10:14.238 --> 00:10:18.295
because deleting the assignment
doesn't remove those resources.

157
00:10:18.295 --> 00:10:22.558
Nothing else is lost; the state,
runs and evidence are kept.

158
00:10:22.558 --> 00:10:25.890
If test keeps running,
register the schedule again.

159
00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:35.215
Before production comes the go/no-go, and
you promote only when every row passes.

160
00:10:35.215 --> 00:10:37.340
The test stage's evidence passes.

161
00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.478
Every provider's collection
matched its own command.

162
00:10:40.478 --> 00:10:42.015
The smoke test passed.

163
00:10:42.015 --> 00:10:47.453
The routing was reviewed, and every security
advisory and retirement became a task.

164
00:10:47.453 --> 00:10:51.228
The alert policy was applied,
and its rollback rehearsed.

165
00:10:51.228 --> 00:10:53.714
Every provider's notice
contacts are named.

166
00:10:53.714 --> 00:10:57.276
And the known gaps are recorded
with the security officer:

167
00:10:57.276 --> 00:11:01.505
Google's planned maintenance, any
AWS account without a support plan,

168
00:11:01.505 --> 00:11:05.808
any workload in an Assured Workloads
FedRAMP or impact-level package,

169
00:11:05.808 --> 00:11:08.553
and what the guide lists
as not yet specified.

170
00:11:08.553 --> 00:11:12.300
The security officer and the
board record the decision.

171
00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:20.807
Stage 2 is production, with the same
commands and the production settings file:

172
00:11:20.807 --> 00:11:25.125
sending off for now, and the alert
policy scoped to the management group.

173
00:11:25.125 --> 00:11:27.855
The pre-flight now includes
the seventh check,

174
00:11:27.855 --> 00:11:31.662
so production refuses to start
unless the test evidence passes.

175
00:11:31.662 --> 00:11:35.787
Enable the providers one at a time
again, and keep routing a dry run for

176
00:11:35.787 --> 00:11:39.912
the first week, while the service
desk works from the payloads by hand.

177
00:11:39.912 --> 00:11:44.738
Apply the alerts at the management group,
schedule the weekly run, and smoke test.

178
00:11:44.738 --> 00:11:49.176
Turn on sending only after that week,
with the change manager's approval.

179
00:11:49.176 --> 00:11:51.012
Each payload is sent once.

180
00:11:51.012 --> 00:11:54.100
Then check the production
evidence every week.

181
00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:02.231
Each step ends with the federal
requirements it supports, checked against

182
00:12:02.231 --> 00:12:06.612
NIST's control catalog, and the FedRAMP
key security indicator it aligns to.

183
00:12:06.612 --> 00:12:09.612
Those indicators belong
to the cloud providers.

184
00:12:09.612 --> 00:12:14.146
The agency authorizes its own system
and lines its evidence up with what its

185
00:12:14.146 --> 00:12:17.600
providers report; it doesn't
report the indicators itself,

186
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.550
and every alignment here
is the program's reading.

187
00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.387
And the evidence is
produced, not only named.

188
00:12:23.387 --> 00:12:26.517
Every script writes a run
folder with a manifest,

189
00:12:26.517 --> 00:12:28.787
and each is recorded in a hash chain.

190
00:12:28.787 --> 00:12:32.298
Each stage's evidence folder
is kept on agency storage,

191
00:12:32.298 --> 00:12:37.270
with the change record, the plan hashes
and the weekly digests beside it.

192
00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:44.033
What breaks in a deployment,
and what guards against it?

193
00:12:44.033 --> 00:12:48.038
Production before test passed puts
the problems in front of the people

194
00:12:48.038 --> 00:12:51.569
who rely on the notices; the
seventh pre-flight check refuses it.

195
00:12:51.569 --> 00:12:56.045
Enabling every provider at once
lets one failure hide the others.

196
00:12:56.045 --> 00:13:00.095
Sending on day one puts duplicate
or misrouted tasks in the queue.

197
00:13:00.095 --> 00:13:03.608
A plan changed after approval
is refused by the hash.

198
00:13:03.608 --> 00:13:06.645
A secret in the settings
file is refused outright.

199
00:13:06.645 --> 00:13:10.017
A scheduled run that loses
a sign-in stops silently,

200
00:13:10.017 --> 00:13:12.345
so the weekly evidence check fails.

201
00:13:12.345 --> 00:13:18.040
And remediation's resources are never
deleted blindly: the change record decides.

202
00:13:21.790 --> 00:13:23.140
Here's what done looks like.

203
00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:28.202
One settings file per stage, with no
secrets, filed with the change record.

204
00:13:28.202 --> 00:13:29.785
The test pre-flight passed,

205
00:13:29.785 --> 00:13:33.453
and every provider was enabled
and cross-checked one at a time.

206
00:13:33.453 --> 00:13:34.991
The smoke test passed.

207
00:13:34.991 --> 00:13:39.290
Both plans were approved by hash,
applied and recorded in test.

208
00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:41.007
The test evidence passed,

209
00:13:41.007 --> 00:13:45.015
and the security officer and
the board recorded the go/no-go.

210
00:13:45.015 --> 00:13:49.523
Production's pre-flight passed the
seventh check, production routed as

211
00:13:49.523 --> 00:13:53.878
a dry run for a week before sending,
and its evidence passes every week.

212
00:13:53.878 --> 00:13:56.503
That's the end of Building
Provider Notices.

213
00:13:56.503 --> 00:13:57.670
Thanks for watching.
